/* ==============================================================
   01 — Robot variables (beta)
   --------------------------------------------------------------
   ROBOT     Meca500 only (R3 or R4)
   TEACHES   CreateVariable, SetVariable, vars. and *vars.
   NEEDS     Robot activated + homed, firmware 11.x
   WATCH     A motion command with no coordinates in it
   TIME      ~25 s
   ============================================================== */

/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THE GAP THIS FILLS

   Everything so far has had its numbers typed into it. That is
   fine for a demo and wrong for a product: a cell that runs three
   part variants should not need three programs that differ only
   in coordinates.

   The robot can hold named, persistent VARIABLES, and motion
   commands can take their arguments from them.

       CreateVariable(name, value, cyclicId, override, volatile)
       SetVariable(name, value)
       GetVariable(name)
       DeleteVariable(name)
       ListVariables()

   BETA

   Say so. This is documented as a beta feature, and an
   integrator planning a machine build deserves to know that
   before they design around it. It works, it is in the manual,
   and the API may still move.

   VALUES

   A variable holds any basic JSON type — boolean, number,
   string, or array. Not an object. JSON rules apply, so booleans
   are lowercase true and false.

   Names are case sensitive and can carry dotted group prefixes,
   which is how you keep a real cell tidy:
   "pick.trayA.approach" rather than fourteen loose names.

   The type is fixed when the variable is created. SetVariable
   will refuse a value of a different type or a different array
   length, and the robot performs no conversion. To change a
   type, create it again with the override argument.

   USING THEM: TWO PREFIXES

     vars.name    a single value
                  SetPayload(vars.part.mass, 0, 0, 30)

     *vars.name   an array, UNROLLED into separate arguments
                  MovePose(*vars.pick.pose)

   The asterisk is the part people miss. A pose is six numbers;
   storing it as one array of six and unrolling it with * is far
   better than six separate variables.

   PERSISTENCE

   Variables are stored on the robot and survive a reboot. The
   suggested pattern, straight from the manual, is one program
   whose only job is to create the variables, run once at
   commissioning — and then production programs that only refer
   to them.

   Note the warning that goes with this: setting a variable is a
   blocking operation that writes to persistent storage. Use
   robot variables for values that change per batch or per
   recipe. Do NOT use them for values that change every cycle —
   those belong in the host program.

   IF YOUR EDITOR FLAGS THIS FILE

   The mecademic-mxprog VS Code extension (v0.1.0) reports errors
   on every line below that uses a variable. They are false
   positives: the extension does not yet model the vars. and
   *vars. prefixes, and it counts a [1, 2, 3] array literal as
   three separate arguments. The syntax here follows the
   manual's own examples. Ignore the squiggles, or check against
   the Variable management commands chapter.

   AND THE HONEST LIMIT

   Variables give you parameters, not logic. There is still no
   IF and no WHILE. You cannot compute a new pose on the robot,
   or choose between two of them based on a sensor. The host does
   that, and then either sets a variable or sends the command.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */


/* --- Part 1: create the variables ------------------------------
   In a real cell this block lives in its own program, run once.
   It is here so this file works standalone.

   override = 1 so the file can be run repeatedly without
   failing on names that already exist. */

/* --- Standard reset: start from a known state -----------------
   Deactivating loses every NON-persistent setting — tool and
   world frames, speeds, accelerations, blending, payload, torque
   limits, posture and turn selection, move mode — so this
   program starts from firmware defaults no matter what ran
   before it. Reactivating brings the motors back.

   This does NOT move the arm: a robot that was already homed
   needs no re-homing. (With an MEGP 25* gripper fitted, the
   gripper re-homes on reactivation. The robot still does not
   move — but keep the fingers clear.)

   It does NOT reset persistent settings — joint limits, work
   zone, tool sphere, collision and calibration config all
   survive. Run common/cold_start.mxprog for those.

   This block is identical in every example. To remove it
   everywhere, see the README. */
DeactivateRobot()
ActivateRobot()
Home()
ResetError()
ResumeMotion()


// Cartesian poses, stored as arrays of six.
CreateVariable("demo.home",     [190, 0, 308.9, 0, 90, 0], 0, 1)
CreateVariable("demo.approach", [190, 0, 290, 0, 90, 0],   0, 1)
CreateVariable("demo.pick",     [190, 0, 250, 0, 90, 0],   0, 1)
CreateVariable("demo.place",    [190, 60, 250, 0, 90, 0],  0, 1)

// Single values: the process parameters a customer would tune.
CreateVariable("demo.travelVel", 200, 0, 1)
CreateVariable("demo.processVel", 25, 0, 1)
CreateVariable("demo.dwell", 0.5, 0, 1)


/* --- Part 2: a program with no coordinates in it ---------------
   Read the motion block below. There is not a single number in
   it. Every position and every speed comes from a variable.

   That is the whole idea: this is now a description of the
   SEQUENCE, and the numbers are data that lives somewhere else
   and can be changed without touching it. */

SetJointVel(20)

SetCartLinVel(vars.demo.travelVel)
MovePose(*vars.demo.home)

MovePose(*vars.demo.approach)

SetCartLinVel(vars.demo.processVel)
MoveLin(*vars.demo.pick)
Delay(vars.demo.dwell)

SetCartLinVel(vars.demo.travelVel)
MovePose(*vars.demo.approach)
MovePose(*vars.demo.place)
Delay(vars.demo.dwell)

MovePose(*vars.demo.home)


/* --- Part 3: changeover ----------------------------------------
   A different part, a different pick location, a slower process
   speed. Three lines of data — and the motion block above is
   reused unchanged.

   In production these SetVariable calls come from the host, from
   a recipe file, or from an operator screen. Nobody edits a
   robot program to change a part. */

SetVariable("demo.pick", [190, -60, 250, 0, 90, 0])
SetVariable("demo.place", [190, 60, 230, 0, 90, 0])
SetVariable("demo.processVel", 10)

// The same sequence again, now running the second part.
MovePose(*vars.demo.approach)
SetCartLinVel(vars.demo.processVel)
MoveLin(*vars.demo.pick)
Delay(vars.demo.dwell)
SetCartLinVel(vars.demo.travelVel)
MovePose(*vars.demo.approach)
MovePose(*vars.demo.place)
Delay(vars.demo.dwell)
MovePose(*vars.demo.home)


// --- Home -----------------------------------------------------
SetCartLinVel(150)
MoveJoints(0, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0)


/* ---------------------------------------------------------------
   THINGS TO TRY

   1. Scroll through part 2 and point out that there are no
      coordinates. Then run part 3 and show the same code running
      a different part. This is the answer to "how do you handle
      changeover?".

   2. Run ListVariables() on its own to show what the robot is
      holding, and GetVariable(demo.pick) to read one back. The
      host can do both over TCP/IP, which is how an HMI screen
      gets built.

   3. Reboot the robot and read the variable again. It is still
      there. Persistence is often the detail that decides whether
      this is useful to them.

   NEXT   02_program_calls.mxprog — structure without flow
          control.
   --------------------------------------------------------------- */
